{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:19457638 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:19457638 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":6,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"19457638","source":"MED","pmid":"19457638","pmcid":"PMC2789188","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC2789188"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.jclinepi.2009.03.001","title":"A most stubborn bias: no adjustment method fully resolves confounding by indication in observational studies.","authorString":"Bosco JL, Silliman RA, Thwin SS, Geiger AM, Buist DS, Prout MN, Yood MU, Haque R, Wei F, Lash TL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bosco JL","lastName":"Bosco","firstName":"Jaclyn L F","initials":"JL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Geriatrics Section, Boston University School of Medicine, Boston, MA 02118, USA. jfong@bu.edu"}]}},{"fullName":"Silliman RA","lastName":"Silliman","firstName":"Rebecca A","initials":"RA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9958-8403"}},{"fullName":"Thwin SS","lastName":"Thwin","firstName":"Soe Soe","initials":"SS"},{"fullName":"Geiger AM","lastName":"Geiger","firstName":"Ann M","initials":"AM"},{"fullName":"Buist DS","lastName":"Buist","firstName":"Diana S M","initials":"DS"},{"fullName":"Prout MN","lastName":"Prout","firstName":"Marianne N","initials":"MN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8747-6199"}},{"fullName":"Yood MU","lastName":"Yood","firstName":"Marianne Ulcickas","initials":"MU"},{"fullName":"Haque R","lastName":"Haque","firstName":"Reina","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Wei F","lastName":"Wei","firstName":"Feifei","initials":"F"},{"fullName":"Lash TL","lastName":"Lash","firstName":"Timothy L","initials":"TL"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8747-6199"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9958-8403"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4204-0871"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["recommended_reviews"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"63","journalIssueId":1681278,"dateOfPublication":"2010 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2010,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Epidemiol","title":"Journal of clinical epidemiology","essn":"1878-5921","issn":"0895-4356","nlmid":"8801383","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Epidemiol"},"printPublicationDate":"2010-01-01"},"pubYear":"2010","pageInfo":"64-74","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>To evaluate the effectiveness of methods that control for confounding by indication, we compared breast cancer recurrence rates among women receiving adjuvant chemotherapy with those who did not.<h4>Study design and setting</h4>In a medical record review-based study of breast cancer treatment in older women (n=1798) diagnosed between 1990 and 1994, our crude analysis suggested that adjuvant chemotherapy was positively associated with recurrence (hazard ratio [HR]=2.6; 95% confidence interval [CI]=1.9, 3.5). We expected a protective effect, so postulated that the crude association was confounded by indications for chemotherapy. We attempted to adjust for this confounding by restriction, multivariable regression, propensity scores (PSs), and instrumental variable (IV) methods.<h4>Results</h4>After restricting to women at high risk for recurrence (n=946), chemotherapy was not associated with recurrence (HR=1.1; 95% CI=0.7, 1.6) using multivariable regression. PS adjustment yielded similar results (HR=1.3; 95% CI=0.8, 2.0). The IV-like method yielded a protective estimate (HR=0.9; 95% CI=0.2, 4.3); however, imbalances of measured factors across levels of the IV suggested residual confounding.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Conventional methods do not control for unmeasured factors, which often remain important when addressing confounding by indication. PS and IV analysis methods can be useful under specific situations, but neither method adequately controlled confounding by indication in this study.","affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Geriatrics Section, Boston University School of Medicine, Boston, MA 02118, USA. jfong@bu.edu","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Evaluation Study","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"K05 CA092395","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R01 CA093772","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Breast Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Recurrence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Antineoplastic Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Treatment Outcome"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chemotherapy, Adjuvant","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Data Interpretation, Statistical"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Propensity Score"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Bias"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Confounding Factors, Epidemiologic"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Antineoplastic Agents","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/pmc2789188?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Subscription required","avai